# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Their Own Words v2 content — Acts I & II (deepened originals + second-cell
questions). Consumed by _build_learn_data_v2.py. Excerpts are referenced by
candidate index (cand:N -> 03-data/learn-quote-candidates.json) or by slice
anchors into the corpus — never retyped. Every entry carries the v2 `context`
editorial layer: this writer's war, and why these words."""

# Each entry: qid, act, theme, source ('cand', N) or ('slice', letterId, startAnchor, endAnchor),
# recip, date_str, stem, choices, answer, whyRight, whyWrong{}, expansion, context, extra(optional)

ACTS_1_2 = [

# ═══════════════ ACT I — "Leaving Home" ═══════════════

dict(qid='a1-nation-1', act=1, theme='nation', source=('cand', 0), recip='to his mother', date_str='July 21, 1861',
  stem='Henry wrote this on July 21, 1861. What battle could he hear from Washington?',
  choices=['The bombardment of Fort Sumter', 'The First Battle of Bull Run', 'The Battle of Antietam', 'The siege of Richmond'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. He was listening to First Bull Run — the war's first great battle, fought at Manassas Junction, close enough to Washington that picnicking spectators rode out to watch and a farm boy in camp could hear the guns.",
  whyWrong={'0': "Fort Sumter had fallen three months earlier, in April — and it's in Charleston harbor, far beyond earshot of Washington. The guns Henry heard were much closer.",
            '2': "Antietam comes fourteen months later, in Maryland — a battle this family will have terrible cause to remember. In July 1861 the war hadn't yet touched them.",
            '3': "Richmond was never besieged in 1861 — the war was four days old in earnest. The firing was at a creek called Bull Run, not thirty miles off."},
  expansion="The Union defeat at Bull Run ended the summer's illusion of a ninety-day war. Nearly 900 men died in an afternoon — more Americans than had fallen in any battle in the nation's history to that point — and the raw Union army broke and streamed back into Washington in the rain, tangled among the carriages of spectators who had come out with picnic baskets. Within days Congress authorized a million three-year volunteers; within weeks George McClellan arrived to build a real army out of the wreckage. Every assumption the country carried into the summer — short war, one battle, home by harvest — died in the hearing of men like Henry, standing in camp, counting the cannon.",
  context="Henry was the first Hubbell to enlist — he walked into the 34th New York in the spring of 1861, weeks after Fort Sumter, and by July he was five hundred miles from the farm at Champlain. Notice what his sentence does NOT do: it doesn't panic. 'While I am writing there is a battle going on' — he keeps the pen moving, half reporter and half boy at the window, misspelling 'hear' as 'here' the way he talks. This is the collection's first brush with real war, and its writer has fourteen months to live. The distance between this letter's calm curiosity and what the family will know by October 1862 is the whole arc of Act I and II — and one reason his early letters are precious: they preserve the war before anyone knew what it was."),

dict(qid='a1-camp-1', act=1, theme='camp', source=('cand', 15), recip='to his mother', date_str='March 6, 1862',
  stem='Alexander wants a message passed to three hometown boys thinking of enlisting. What is he telling them?',
  choices=['Bring warm clothes — the army never issues enough', 'Stay home — the war is already lost', 'Soldiering is drudgery that will cure their romantic ideas fast', 'Join the cavalry — it pays better than the infantry'],
  answer=2,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. “All the poetry will be taken out of them” — the gap between the parade-ground fantasy and the mud was the first lesson every recruit learned.",
  whyWrong={'1': "Not at all — Alexander never doubted the cause; he reenlisted as a veteran and served to the very end. His warning is about romance, not defeat.",
            '3': "He mentions cavalry — but as the men he watched drilling through mud and water, not as an upgrade. No branch escaped the drudgery."},
  expansion="Soldiers called their first combat “seeing the elephant,” but the disillusionment started long before any battle: drill by the hour, mud, lice, bad water, spoiled rations, and boredom thick enough to cut. An army of farm boys and clerks had to be manufactured into soldiers, and the winter of 1861–62 was the factory floor — McClellan drilled the Army of the Potomac six days a week while the newspapers demanded he fight with it. Disease did more damage than drill: measles, mumps, and typhoid tore through camps full of rural men meeting crowd diseases for the first time. The romantic recruit of 1861 was a stock figure in soldiers' letters everywhere, always described the same way — as somebody the writer used to be.",
  context="Alexander is barely six months a soldier here, and already writing like a veteran — that's the joke and the point. He enlisted at seventeen in September 1861, the second brother in, and spent his first winter garrisoning Baltimore, a city that had stoned Union troops the spring before. The swagger in this sentence ('come down HERE and begin to drill') is a younger brother's swagger: Dodds, Beaumont, and Bowen are boys his own age, and he wants them to know he has crossed a line they haven't. Watch this voice across the game — it deepens at Antietam, hardens at Gettysburg, and by 1865 it writes the collection's great valediction. The poetry gets taken out of him too; what replaces it is worth more."),

dict(qid='a1-body-1', act=1, theme='body', source=('cand', 4), recip='to his sister Fannie', date_str='February 5, 1862',
  stem='Henry describes his first night on campaign: no tent, February cold, a single blanket. What does this kind of exposure explain about Civil War deaths?',
  choices=['Why frostbite was the leading cause of discharge', 'Why roughly two of every three soldier deaths came from disease, not battle', 'Why armies refused to campaign in winter', 'Why the Union issued fur uniforms after 1862'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. Battle killed spectacularly, but exposure, bad water, and crowded camps killed steadily — disease took about twice as many soldiers as combat did.",
  whyWrong={'2': "Armies did prefer winter quarters — but campaigns happened in every season (Henry is ON one, in February). The real cost of cold wasn't paused wars; it was filled hospitals.",
            '3': "No fur uniforms ever came. The soldier's issue stayed wool and canvas — which is exactly why nights like this one mattered."},
  expansion="Of roughly 360,000 Union dead, some 250,000 were killed not by Confederates but by disease — pneumonia, typhoid, dysentery, measles — and the arithmetic was the same in gray. The killers worked in sequence: exposure and exhaustion lowered a man's defenses, camp crowding delivered the microbe, and 1860s medicine — which did not yet know germs existed — could do little but watch. Rural recruits died at higher rates than city men because they'd never met childhood diseases that urban soldiers already survived. A night on frozen ground with one blanket wasn't an anecdote; it was the first link in the chain that filled the war's hospitals. The armies eventually learned — better camps, better sinks, vegetables when they could get them — but the ledger never balanced: the Civil War remained, above all, a public-health catastrophe wearing a uniform.",
  context="Henry is writing to Fannie, his sister — and to her he's more vivid than to his mother, letting the hardship show ('Sleep was of course out of the question'). The 34th New York had just been pushed up to Bolivar Heights above Harpers Ferry in winter — his first real taste of campaigning after months of guard duty. Read the understatement in 'of course': five words that normalize misery, written by a twenty-four-year-old who grew up with feather beds and a woodstove. This family will live the statistic this question teaches — the war kills three of the four brothers, one in battle and two by what it does to their bodies — and it starts on nights exactly like this one."),

dict(qid='a1-purse-1', act=1, theme='purse', source=('cand', 3), recip='to his mother', date_str='February 4, 1862',
  stem="Henry plans to “keep clear” of the sutler and save $26 over two months. What does that tell you a Union private earned?",
  choices=['About $13 a month', 'About $26 a week', 'A dollar a day, like a farmhand', 'Nothing — privates served without pay'],
  answer=0,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. Thirteen dollars a month — and Henry's arithmetic (two months, $26) is the primary source doing the teaching.",
  whyWrong={'1': "That would make a private rich — $26 was two full months of army pay, which is exactly why Henry frames saving it as an achievement.",
            '2': "A northern farmhand might earn that, which is the point — army pay was a wage CUT for most of these men, taken anyway."},
  expansion="The sutler was a licensed private merchant attached to each regiment, selling pies, cheese, tobacco, paper, and small luxuries at legendary markups — often on credit against payday, with the debt collected at the pay table before the soldier touched a cent. Since the paymaster might not appear for months (pay ran chronically late all war), a careless private could live in permanent debt to a wagon. Thirteen dollars a month, when a laborer at home might make twenty-five or thirty: the army ran on men taking a pay cut to serve, and the difference between the soldiers who sent money home and the ones who owed the sutler was a running moral drama in every company street. Congress finally raised pay to $16 in mid-1864 — after three years of inflation had already eaten the raise.",
  context="Henry writes about money constantly, and almost always in one direction: home. The Hubbells farmed on the Canadian border; cash was tight and sons' wages mattered. 'Come out with $26.00 on next pay day' isn't budgeting for its own sake — two lines later in letters like this one, that money is promised to Champlain. Every brother runs the same economy (watch Charles's sutler warnings in Act III), and their mother's side of the correspondence tracks the remittances like a ledger. The war, for this family, was among other things a cash-flow problem — and the discipline in this sentence, written by the eldest at twenty-four, is him being a provider from five hundred miles away."),

dict(qid='a1-family-1', act=1, theme='family', source=('cand', 14), recip='to his mother', date_str='January 24, 1862',
  stem='Alexander repeats one word three times in a single sentence. Why did mail matter this much to soldiers?',
  choices=['Letters were the only proof to the army that a soldier had a family', 'Letters from home were the emotional lifeline of the ranks — and officers knew it', 'Soldiers were paid a bonus for every letter received', 'Regulations required a weekly letter to remain in good standing'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. “Write often, write often, often” — no line in the collection says it plainer. Mail call decided the mood of a company.",
  whyWrong={'2': "No bonus existed — if anything, soldiers PAID to write: paper and stamps were scarce, and some letters went home marked postage-due.",
            '3': "No such regulation existed. The pressure ran the other way — homesick soldiers begging their families to write faster."},
  expansion="The armies moved mail on a scale no American institution ever had — some 180,000 letters a day passed through Washington alone at the war's height. The U.S. Christian Commission and Sanitary Commission handed out free stationery by the ton; sutlers sold patriotic envelopes; and Congress let soldiers send letters postage-due so an empty pocket never silenced a man. Officers treated mail as materiel: a regiment that heard from home fought better, deserted less, and sickened less, and every commander knew it. The result is the richest written record any war had produced to that date — millions of ordinary voices, of which this archive's 272 are one family's thread.",
  context="This is Alexander at seventeen, four months into service, and the repetition isn't style — it's need. His Baltimore winter was garrison boredom in a hostile city, and the mail was the only part of the day that could surprise him. The plea worked: Frances Hubbell wrote, and kept writing, and KEPT what came back — which is why you're reading any of this. That's worth sitting with: this archive exists because a mother obeyed this exact sentence for four years. When the letters stop mattering — when the family is finally reunited at a tea table in 1865 — the record simply ends. The game's last question teaches that lesson; this one is its seed."),

dict(qid='a1-camp-2', act=1, theme='camp', source=('cand', 2), recip='to his mother', date_str='November 30, 1861',
  stem='Henry describes the army settling in for its first winter. What were Civil War “winter quarters”?',
  choices=['Heated barracks built by the War Department in northern cities', 'Log-and-canvas huts the soldiers built themselves, whole armies pausing until spring', 'Requisitioned civilian houses along the march route', 'Tents issued double-thick for the season'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. “We shall probably have log houses” — the men were their own carpenters. Each winter, armies became sprawling self-built cities of huts and mud streets, and mostly stopped fighting until the roads dried.",
  whyWrong={'0': "The War Department built almost nothing of the kind — a Civil War army housed itself with axes, hardtack boxes, and whatever timber stood nearby.",
            '2': "Requisitioning happened in the South as the war hardened, but an army of 100,000 couldn't winter in farmhouses — it built its own town instead."},
  expansion="Campaigning in the 1860s obeyed the roads, and unpaved roads obeyed the weather: when winter rains turned Virginia to glue, both armies dug in until spring. The men paired off and built 'shebangs' — log walls chinked with mud, canvas or plank roofs, barrel-and-clay chimneys that regularly caught fire — arranged in company streets complete with names like Broadway. Winter quarters had its own rhythms: drill shrank, letter-writing exploded, revivals and snowball fights (whole brigades, with casualties) filled the weeks, and boredom and disease did the season's real fighting. The pause was strategic fact — Lincoln and the newspapers raged at armies 'all quiet' for months — but no general could march a wagon train through February mud, and every soldier knew it.",
  context="Henry writes this in the collection's first winter, when everything is still new enough to describe — log houses are a curiosity worth telling his mother about, almost cheerful, like a boys' camping expedition that got out of hand. That's the texture of late 1861 in these letters: the war as an arrangement of practical problems (housing, food, cold) rather than violence. It will not survive the spring. Within four months the 34th New York is on the Peninsula, and Henry's letters trade carpentry for casualty lists. The historians' name for what these early letters capture is the 'phony war' of the North's first winter — a million men under arms and almost nobody shooting — and Henry's log houses are it in one sentence."),

dict(qid='a1-nation-2', act=1, theme='nation', source=('cand', 13), recip='to his mother', date_str='January 17, 1862',
  stem='Alexander insists he “did not enlist only for the novelty of the thing.” What actually drove most Union volunteers of 1861?',
  choices=['Enlistment bounties — the money was too good to refuse', 'Conscription — most had no choice', 'Conviction: preserving the Union, duty, and proving their manhood to the folks at home', 'Promises of western land grants after the war'],
  answer=2,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. The men of '61 were volunteers in the fullest sense — they enlisted before big bounties existed and before any draft, moved by Union, duty, community pressure, and the need to be seen doing right.",
  whyWrong={'0': "The big bounties come later, in 1862–64, when volunteering slowed. The men of 1861 got $13 a month and a send-off from the town — that's all.",
            '1': "There was no draft until 1862 (state militia) and 1863 (federal). Every man in the ranks in January 1862 had signed of his own accord."},
  expansion="Historians have read tens of thousands of soldiers' letters asking exactly this question, and the answer of 1861 is remarkably consistent: the men wrote about the Union — the inheritance of the Revolution, 'the best government on earth' — about duty, and about what the town would think of them. Ideology and honor braided together: to stay home while neighbors went was to be marked. Emancipation barely appears in northern enlistment letters of 1861; it enters the ranks' vocabulary later, as the war hardens and soldiers see slavery up close. That evolution — from 'Union' in 1861 to 'free and united' by 1865 — is one of the great measurable changes in American thought, and it happens inside ordinary men. This game ends on Alexander's own version of it.",
  context="Alexander is defending his enlistment to his mother here — a seventeen-year-old assuring her it wasn't a lark, drawing a pointed contrast with some other boy who (he claims) joined for the novelty. There's self-fashioning in it: he NEEDS her to see him as serious. But the war called his bluff and he answered — through Antietam's aftermath, Gettysburg, Lookout Mountain, Atlanta, four full years, reenlisting when his term ran out. Of the four brothers, Alexander alone serves from 1861 to the surrender. Whatever mixture of motives carried him in, what carried him THROUGH is on display across this whole game — and it ends with his own summary of what it was all for."),

# ═══════════════ ACT II — "The Hardening" ═══════════════

dict(qid='a2-camp-1', act=2, theme='camp', source=('cand', 37), recip='to his mother', date_str='October 15, 1862',
  stem='Charles, three weeks a soldier, rides south in a rail car with guarded doors. Why were the doors guarded?',
  choices=['To keep Confederate spies from boarding', 'To stop new recruits from slipping away en route', 'To protect the payroll chest aboard the train', 'Quarantine — measles was loose in the regiment'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. Bounty men and cold-footed recruits vanished at every water stop — so the 153rd New York rode south under guard, thirsty.",
  whyWrong={'0': "Spies were a real fear, but not what door guards on a recruit train were for — the threat these guards watched was on the inside.",
            '3': "Disease did stalk new regiments (measles especially) — but quarantine meant camps, not locked cars. The guards were for the men, not the microbes."},
  expansion="The Civil War was the first war moved by rail at scale: a thousand-man regiment with baggage and beef could shift hundreds of miles in days instead of weeks, and the Union's 22,000 miles of track (to the Confederacy's 9,000) was a war-winning asymmetry. But the same trains leaked men. By late 1862 enlistment bounties had created the 'bounty jumper' — enlist, collect, vanish at the first water stop, repeat in the next county — and even honest recruits wobbled when the cars actually started moving away from home. So new regiments traveled like valuable, volatile cargo: doors guarded, canteens empty, sergeants counting heads at every halt. Charles's thirsty complaint is the logistics revolution and its human friction in a single sentence.",
  context="This is Charles's first letter as a soldier — twenty years old, three weeks in uniform, still inside New York State, and already the 153rd's discipline has announced itself: no water at supper, doors guarded. He enlisted in the terrible autumn of 1862, weeks after Henry disappeared at Antietam — which means this family sent TWO more sons (James follows) into the army while still searching casualty lists for the first. Charles's voice, here at the start, is exactly what it stays for three years: dry, precise, a little wry about institutional absurdity ('which went well enough, but…'). Of the three surviving brothers, he's the one who reports the war rather than performs it — and it makes him the collection's most reliable witness."),

dict(qid='a2-body-1', act=2, theme='body', source=('cand', 7), recip='to his brother Charles', date_str='June 13, 1862',
  stem='Henry is describing the ground after Fair Oaks, outside Richmond. Why were Civil War battles this lethal?',
  choices=['Rifled muskets met tactics designed for older, shorter-range weapons', 'Neither army took prisoners in 1862', 'Artillery had become accurate beyond a mile', 'Most of the dead were victims of hand-to-hand fighting'],
  answer=0,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. The rifle-musket was deadly at three times the range of the old smoothbore — but men still advanced shoulder-to-shoulder, as the manuals said. The arithmetic landed on fields like this one.",
  whyWrong={'1': "Both sides took prisoners throughout 1862 — parole and exchange were routine. The dead Henry saw fell to firepower, not to a no-quarter war.",
            '3': "Bayonet wounds accounted for well under one percent of casualties. The killing happened at range — which is exactly what made the old close-order tactics so costly."},
  expansion="The rifle-musket — a Springfield or Enfield throwing a conical Minié ball — was accurate to 300 yards and dangerous well past that, three times the reach of the smoothbores the tactics manuals were written for. But the manuals still ruled: close-order lines, massed volleys, frontal assault. The result was a fifty-year gap between weapon and doctrine, paid in bodies. Fair Oaks (Seven Pines) cost the two armies about eleven thousand men in two days of confused fighting in flooded woods — and it was only a preview: within three weeks the Seven Days battles would quadruple it. It was also the battle that gave the South Robert E. Lee, when Joseph Johnston fell wounded and command changed hands. The armies eventually adapted — trenches, skirmish order, the spade — but 1862 was the year the arithmetic taught itself.",
  context="Read who this letter is addressed to: Charles — brother to brother, not son to mother. In the same week Henry writes home about longing for a good meal; to his brother he writes 'the ground was covered with the dead,' plain as weather. The brothers kept a second, harder war for each other, and the split runs through the whole collection: reassurance flowed to Champlain, reality flowed between the tents. Henry has eleven weeks to live when he writes this. And Charles — the recipient — will enlist within three months of reading it, knowing exactly what the ground looks like. That's the fact worth carrying out of this question: the letters didn't just record the war; they recruited for it, honestly, inside one family."),

dict(qid='a2-purse-1', act=2, theme='purse', source=('cand', 20), recip='to his mother and sister', date_str='November 25, 1862',
  stem='Alexander is scoffing at two men from his hometown. What had they done?',
  choices=['Bribed officials to lose their enlistment papers', 'Hired other men to serve in the army in their place', 'Bought exemptions from the local sheriff', 'Enlisted, then paid to be assigned safe garrison duty'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. Under the militia drafts — and later the 1863 Enrollment Act — a drafted man could send a paid substitute instead. “Give them the slip” is Alexander wishing the substitutes would take the money and vanish.",
  whyWrong={'2': "Exemption corruption existed, but it's not what he names — he says they're “paying and hoarding substitutes”: hiring replacement men and keeping them ready.",
            '0': "Nothing so furtive — what they did was perfectly legal, which is precisely why it stung the men in the ranks."},
  expansion="Substitution was old militia custom carried into an industrial war: a man selected in a draft could hire another to go in his stead, and by late 1862 New York's militia draft had turned able-bodied replacements into a commodity — 'paying and hoarding,' as Alexander puts it, like grain against a price rise. The federal Enrollment Act of 1863 kept the practice and added the $300 commutation fee beside it, and together they fed the war's bitterest slogan: 'a rich man's war and a poor man's fight.' The grievance was somewhat unfair in aggregate — most soldiers were volunteers, and substitute prices sat within reach of skilled workers — but fairness in aggregate never soothed a man in the ranks reading hometown news of who had bought his way out. Grant later called the substitute market a source of the army's worst soldiers; the men it replaced had opinions too, and Alexander's is preserved here in its original temperature.",
  context="Twelve days before this letter, Alexander walked out of the Antietam campaign's hospital circuit; eight weeks before it, his brother Henry vanished into that battle. Now comes word from Champlain that Whiteside and Doolittle — men of means, names his mother would say with a certain tone — are stockpiling substitutes. Read the punctuation: the quotation marks around 'paying and hoarding substitutes' are him holding the phrase at arm's length like something spoiled, and 'give them the slip just when wanted' is as close to a curse as he allows himself in a letter his sister will read. This is Thanksgiving week, 1862; the same letter carries his account of surviving Antietam by God's kindness. The two sentiments — gratitude and class fury — sit four paragraphs apart, and both are the war."),

dict(qid='a2-nation-1', act=2, theme='nation', source=('cand', 10), recip='to his mother', date_str='July 12, 1862',
  stem='Henry watched the Irish Brigade charge on the Peninsula. What does that unit tell you about the Union army?',
  choices=['It relied heavily on immigrant soldiers, some in famous ethnic regiments', 'It hired foreign mercenaries under European officers', 'Only U.S. citizens were allowed to fight', 'Irish units served only as labor battalions'],
  answer=0,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. Roughly one Union soldier in four was foreign-born — Irish, German, Scandinavian — and units like Meagher's Irish Brigade carried their identity into battle, green flags and all.",
  whyWrong={'1': "No mercenaries — these were volunteers and citizens-in-the-making; many enlisted partly to prove their Americanness.",
            '2': "Citizenship was never required — declaring intent was enough, and the army became a fast road to naturalization."},
  expansion="The immigration waves of the 1840s and '50s — Irish famine refugees, Germans fleeing failed revolutions — had remade the North, and the army showed it: about a quarter of Union soldiers were born abroad, and whole regiments drilled in German. Thomas Francis Meagher's Irish Brigade fought under emerald flags with a reputation for exactly the kind of charge Henry describes, paying for it at Antietam's Bloody Lane and Fredericksburg's stone wall at rates that broke the brigade twice over. Immigrant service was also an argument: nativist politics had spent a decade calling these men un-American, and they answered at places whose names ended up on their flags. When the draft riots exploded in Irish New York a year later, the brigade's casualty lists were the counter-fact — the same community was buying the Union's victory at both ends.",
  context="Henry watches the charge like a man at a spectacle — 'it was a splendid sight' — and there's no distance or condescension in it, just a farm boy's honest awe at other men's courage. Champlain, New York, sits on the Canadian border, about as far from immigrant Manhattan as northern society got; the Army of the Potomac was likely the first place Henry ever stood beside men shouting in brogues and singing in German. That's a quiet, real function of the Union army historians point to: it was the largest mixing chamber the country had ever run, and letters like this one carried the mixture home. Two months after writing this, Henry fell at Antietam — the same battle where the brigade he admired lost 540 men in the Bloody Lane."),

dict(qid='a2-family-1', act=2, theme='family', source=('cand', 58), recip='to Alexander', date_str='October 29, 1862',
  stem='Six weeks after Antietam, Frances still doesn’t know what happened to Henry. Why could a mother be left guessing this long?',
  choices=['The army kept casualty lists secret in wartime', 'There was no official system to identify the dead or notify families', 'Mail from Maryland was blockaded', 'Families were only notified after a soldier’s enlistment expired'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. No dog tags, no graves registration, no notification service — identification depended on comrades, and after a battle like Antietam, comrades were often gone too.",
  whyWrong={'0': "Papers PRINTED casualty lists — that was often how families found out, badly and late. The problem wasn't secrecy; it was that no one was responsible for telling them at all.",
            '2': "Mail moved — her letters reached Alexander at Harpers Ferry. What didn't exist was anyone whose job it was to send the one letter she feared."},
  expansion="The United States entered its bloodiest war with no dog tags, no graves-registration service, no next-of-kin notification, no ambulance corps worth the name — none of the machinery we now assume death in war sets in motion. Identification rested on comrades recognizing a face, a letter in a pocket, initials inked on a waistband; some soldiers pinned paper name-tags to their coats before assaults, inventing the dog tag out of dread. After Antietam — still the bloodiest single day in American history, with more than 3,600 dead — burial details worked for days, and nearly half the Union dead of the entire war ended under boards marked UNKNOWN. Families performed their own searches: writing to colonels, chaplains, hospitals, hiring agents, reading the papers' agate-type lists at the post office window. The scholar Drew Gilpin Faust called the result a 'republic of suffering' — a nation forced to reinvent death itself. Frances Hubbell's morning walks to the post office were that reinvention, one family at a time.",
  context="This is the collection's most terrible stretch of correspondence: Frances writing to her surviving son at Harpers Ferry about the one who has vanished. Look at the specificity of her dread — 'one of the number found in the bushes, who had died of their wounds' — she has been reading battlefield accounts closely enough to furnish her nightmares with terrain. For six weeks she has walked to the post office each morning 'with the hope that I should hear' (her own words from another letter in this archive). She never gets a grave: Henry's body was never identified, and the family's knowledge of his death assembled itself from comrades' letters and official silence. The interlude after this act carries what they finally concluded. Her letters from these weeks are why this archive matters beyond one family — they are what the casualty statistics felt like from a kitchen in Champlain."),

dict(qid='a2-camp-2', act=2, theme='camp', source=('cand', 17), recip='to his mother', date_str='June 17, 1862',
  stem='Alexander jokes that his mother should be “horrified” to have a hen roost robber in the family. What was foraging in the Civil War?',
  choices=['A court-martial offense the army never tolerated', 'Soldiers supplementing rations from the countryside — officially forbidden, universally practiced', 'A paid arrangement between the army and local farmers', 'Something only cavalry units were permitted to do'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. Every army in Virginia lightened the local hen roosts, whatever the general orders said — and Alexander's mock confession to his mother is the practice in its natural voice.",
  whyWrong={'0': "Orders against it were real and occasionally enforced — but a hungry regiment enforced its own policy after dark, and most officers looked away.",
            '2': "Formal requisition with payment existed on paper, especially early in the war. The chickens Alexander means changed hands under no such paperwork."},
  expansion="The army ration — hardtack, salt pork, coffee — kept a man alive and little more, and it arrived irregularly on campaign. The countryside made up the difference: chickens, hogs, orchard fruit, cornfields, honey (bee stings were a recognized foraging hazard), milked cows. Early in the war, Union policy protected southern civilian property, part of a conciliatory strategy to coax the South back; soldiers ignored it with a clean conscience, reasoning that rebels' chickens were fair contraband. As the war hardened, policy caught up with practice — by 1864 Sherman's army in Georgia foraged by ORDER, and 'bummer' became a job description. The hen roost is actually a lens on the war's whole moral trajectory: from guarding rebel property in 1862 to burning the Shenandoah's barns in 1864. Alexander's joke sits at the innocent end of that line.",
  context="This is the collection's comedian at work. Alexander writes to his mother with a wink — 'are you not horrified to think you have a hen roost' — inviting her to scold him and daring her to laugh, twelve days after a forced march up the Shenandoah Valley on half rations. The humor is doing real work: it converts hunger, exhaustion, and petty theft into a story a mother can receive without fear. You'll see him do this again and again — he laughs hardest exactly when things are worst (wait for the 1864 hospital letter where all three surviving brothers are laid up at once, and he calls it funny). Historians of soldiers' letters call this 'cheerfulness discipline'; families depended on it, and mothers read straight through it. Frances surely did."),

dict(qid='a2-body-2', act=2, theme='body', source=('cand', 6), recip='to Charles Hubbell', date_str='June 13, 1862',
  stem='Henry, camped outside Richmond, is dreaming about food. What was the Union soldier’s actual staple diet?',
  choices=['Fresh beef and baked bread from field kitchens', 'Hardtack crackers, salt pork, and coffee', 'Canned goods — the war invented the ration tin', 'Whatever the Sanitary Commission delivered weekly'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. Hardtack — a flour-and-water cracker hard enough to crack teeth — plus salted pork and coffee was the marching ration, weeks on end when the army moved.",
  whyWrong={'0': "Fresh beef existed when cattle herds kept up with the column, and soft bread appeared in fixed camps — but on campaign the haversack held crackers, not bakery.",
            '3': "The Sanitary Commission supplemented hospitals and camps with vegetables and comforts — a lifesaving margin, but never the ration itself."},
  expansion="The official marching ration was about a pound of hardtack, three-quarters of a pound of salt pork or a pound-and-a-quarter of fresh beef, plus coffee, sugar, and salt. Hardtack earned its nicknames — 'tooth-dullers,' 'worm castles' (the weevils were protein, soldiers joked) — and men crumbled it into coffee or fried it in pork grease into 'skillygalee.' The diet's real cost was invisible: months without fresh vegetables brought scurvy, night blindness, and the weakened constitutions that let camp diseases kill. The armies knew vitamin-C mattered without knowing why — 'desiccated vegetables' (compressed dried sheets the men called 'desecrated vegetables') and Sanitary Commission onion and potato shipments were the countermeasures. Diet, more than any single factor, is why disease outkilled battle two to one — and why in Act IV of this game, a veteran soldier outside Atlanta gets a diagnosis you might not expect in the world's best-supplied army.",
  context="'How I long to have one good meal at home, even if it wasn't anything but…' — Henry's hunger reaches for the family table, not a restaurant; the fantasy is domestic, specific, his mother's kitchen. He writes this to Charles the same day he tells him the ground at Fair Oaks was covered with the dead — homesickness and horror sharing one envelope, which is what June 1862 on the Peninsula actually felt like. There's foreshadowing here the family couldn't see: the diet Henry grumbles about will, two years later, put his brother Alexander in an Atlanta hospital with scurvy. When you reach that question in Act IV, remember this one — the same broken supply of vegetables, working on a different brother."),

dict(qid='a2-purse-2', act=2, theme='purse', source=('cand', 9), recip='to his mother', date_str='July 12, 1862',
  stem='Henry tallies what finishing his enlistment will bring: “$100 bounty” plus back pay. What was the bounty system?',
  choices=['Combat pay for soldiers who had seen battle', 'Cash inducements — federal, state, and local — layered on top of wages to drive enlistment', 'A pension paid only to wounded veterans', 'Prize money from captured Confederate property'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. The $100 federal bounty (mostly paid at discharge) was the base layer; states, counties, and towns stacked their own on top as volunteering slowed — by 1864 the pile could reach $1,000.",
  whyWrong={'0': "No combat pay existed — a man under fire at Gaines's Mill and a clerk in Washington drew the same $13.",
            '3': "Naval crews shared prize money from captured ships; an infantry private's chickens (see the hen roost) were as close as he came."},
  expansion="The bounty was the Union's answer to a volunteer war outlasting volunteer enthusiasm. The federal $100 (for three-year men) was enacted in 1861 with most of it held back to discourage desertion; when the bloody summer of 1862 stalled recruiting, Lincoln's July call for 300,000 more men set states, counties, and towns bidding against each other with local bounties on top. The system worked — the men came — and corrupted itself in the same motion: 'bounty jumpers' enlisted, collected, deserted, and re-enlisted elsewhere under new names, some making a career of it (one professional jumped thirty-two times). By 1864 the stacked bounties for a veteran reenlistment could exceed six years of a private's wages. The bounty is the war's purse in miniature: a democracy discovering what it actually had to pay, in cash, for the army its ideals required.",
  context="Henry's accounting here — bounty owed, pay owed, 'if I serve my full time' — is a man planning a future in the middle of the Seven Days' aftermath, at Harrison's Landing where the beaten army licked its wounds. The conditional breaks your heart at this distance: he had ten weeks left. The $100 was ultimately paid the way most of the war's promises to Henry were settled — to his family, after his death, through the paperwork his brother Charles doggedly pursued (you'll meet that pursuit in Act III, when Charles pushes their mother to file for 'Henry's back pay and bounty'). Between this letter and that one runs the whole machinery of a Victorian government learning to owe money to its dead."),

dict(qid='a2-nation-2', act=2, theme='nation', source=('cand', 18), recip='to his mother', date_str='July 26, 1862',
  stem='Alexander unloads on “fireside patriots.” Who is he talking about?',
  choices=['Confederate sympathizers operating openly in the North', 'Loud home-front men, full of war opinions and enlistment advice, who would not enlist themselves', 'Newspaper editors who criticized the generals', 'Politicians who voted against army funding'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. “Brave enough at home” — the phrase soldiers everywhere used for the men who cheered the war from armchairs while the recruiting offices stood empty in the bloody summer of 1862.",
  whyWrong={'0': "Actual disloyalty — Copperheads — drew a different, hotter vocabulary in the ranks (you'll meet it in Act IV). The fireside patriot's sin wasn't treason; it was comfort.",
            '2': "Editors caught their share of soldier scorn, but Alexander's target is nearer home — the neighbors."},
  expansion="July 1862 was the volunteer system's crisis: the Peninsula campaign had failed, casualty lists filled the papers, and Lincoln called for 300,000 more men into a silence that lasted weeks. The gap between home-front war enthusiasm and home-front enlistment became THE running grievance in soldiers' letters — 'stay-at-home rangers,' 'parlor soldiers,' Alexander's 'fireside patriots.' The pressure resolved itself within a month in the carrot-and-stick of big bounties and the first militia draft, which together produced the brothers Charles and James's regiment, the 153rd New York, that autumn. Soldier resentment of the home front never fully healed; it resurfaces in this game when Charles cheers the 1863 draft as justice and when the substitute-hoarding neighbors get their roasting. The Civil War was fought by communities — and audited by them, name by name, in letters exactly like this one.",
  context="Every armchair general in Champlain apparently had advice for the Hubbell boys, and Alexander — eighteen now, a year in uniform, fresh from the Valley campaign's marches — has reached his limit. 'They are brave enough at home' is the sound of a teenager discovering class anger through military service; the same nerve will fire again in November at the substitute-hoarders. Here's the twist worth noticing: within three months of this letter, TWO more Hubbell sons enlist. Whatever shame campaign the fireside patriots deserved, this family kept over-subscribing its own quota — four sons from one widow's farm. If the town needed a standard for who was carrying the war, the address was theirs."),

dict(qid='a2-family-2', act=2, theme='family', source=('cand', 57), recip='to Alexander', date_str='October 2, 1862',
  stem='Frances describes her mornings, two weeks after Antietam. What has she been doing every day?',
  choices=['Organizing relief supplies for the wounded', 'Walking to the post office, hoping for word from or about Henry', 'Petitioning the governor for information', 'Traveling to Washington to search the hospitals'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. “I have been to the post office every morning this week” — the daily walk of every family with a son in the Maryland campaign, made with hope on the way there and managed dread on the way back.",
  whyWrong={'3': "Some families did exactly that — the roads to Sharpsburg and Washington filled with parents after the battle. Frances, on a border farm with a household to hold together, had the post office.",
            '0': "Northern women organized relief on a vast scale (Act IV teaches it) — but these particular mornings belonged to one envelope that didn't come."},
  expansion="News in 1862 traveled by telegraph to the newspapers and by post to the families — in that order. A battle's first casualty lists were newspaper columns, wired in haste, riddled with errors, corrected over days; the personal letter (from the soldier if he could write, from a comrade or chaplain if he couldn't) lagged the papers by days or weeks. So families lived in the gap: the papers said the regiment was engaged, and then — nothing, until the mail resolved it one way or the other. The post office became the war's parish church, visited daily, where the whole town could watch who came out reading and who came out empty. For the tens of thousands of families whose men were never identified, the gap simply never closed — no letter, no grave, only the slow hardening of no-news into knowledge. That is what Act II's interlude carries for this family.",
  context="Two letters from Frances sit in this act, and together they map the geography of home-front grief: this one, October 2nd, still structured by hope — the morning walk, the daily chance — and the October 29th letter (this act's other Frances question) where hope has narrowed to the 'unknown buried.' Between them lie four weeks of mornings. It's worth knowing what she was managing while she walked: a farm, a household, a daughter, and correspondence with three sons still in uniform — Alexander at Harpers Ferry within sight of the battlefield that took Henry, and Charles and James newly enlisted. The archive holds seventeen of her letters, and they are its emotional spine; this game gives her a full column of the matrix because the war ran through her kitchen as surely as it ran through Virginia."),
]
